# Go

## General Practices
- When resolving a linter error, always execute the corresponding unit tests before proceeding to the next linter fix.
- NEVER change manually `go.mod` and `go.sum`, unless explicitly authorized or instructed.
- AVOID using the local `go` installation on the host machine. PREFER to use `rules_go` Go SDK. Example:

Execute: 

```sh
bazel run @rules_go//go -- mod tidy -v
```

Instead of:

```sh
go mod tidy -v
```

## Code Organization
- ALWAYS consider refactoring a function.
- AVOID increasing the scope of a function, prefer to create another one instead.
- Write idiomatic Go. Follow Go proverbs.
- ALWAYS write unit tests and make code testable.
- Group related types and functions together.
- Keep functions focused and small.

## Testing Best Practices
- Use table-driven tests for similar test cases.
- Make tests run in parallel when possible using `t.Parallel()`.
- Create dedicated test types for better organization (e.g., `testCase`, `testEnv`).
- Use helper functions to reduce duplication and improve maintainability.
- Ensure proper cleanup of test resources using `defer`.
- Protect shared resources with mutexes in parallel tests.
- Create isolated test environments for each test when running in parallel.
- Use descriptive test case names that explain the scenario being tested.
- Group test cases by functionality using separate functions.
- For Go 1.22+ modules, loop variable captures (`x := x`) are not needed.

## Libraries

- Use the `make` `go-get` target to add third-party dependencies. Example `DEP=github.com/stretchr/testify make go-get`. After run `bazel mod tidy`.
- Use `github.com/jfrog/jfrog-client-go` to interact with JFrog Artifactory REST APIs.
- Use `github.com/google/go-github` to interact with GitHub APIs, if needed.
- Use `github.com/stretchr/testify` for testing. Prefer to use the `require` package.
- Use `github.com/spf13/viper` if you want to enhance CLI configuration.

## Validation

- Once you are done with code changes, run `make test` and confirm all unit tests are passing.
- Once all unit tests are passing, execute `make lint` and fix the findings before finishing it.
- Repeat this process until both pass.
- When fixing linter issues, prefer refactoring to improve code quality rather than just satisfying the linter.
- Consider extracting long functions into smaller, more focused functions.
- Use meaningful names for extracted functions that describe their purpose.